<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1814 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Star-Spangled Banner</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem Francis Scott Key wrote in 1814 after seeing the American flag still flying over a fort after a battle. It is the US anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Francis Scott Key</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>John Stafford Smith (the tune of &quot;The Anacreontic Song&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Americans of the early United States; the national anthem of the United States</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Baltimore, Maryland, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem written 14 September 1814; printed as a broadside 17 September 1814; national anthem by law in 1931</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Francis Scott Key (1814; he died in 1843))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, after the burning of Washington, British forces arrested William Beanes, a physician of Upper Marlboro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, and a friend of the lawyer Francis Scott Key. With the sanction of President Madison, Key went to Baltimore in early September 1814 and, with John Stuart Skinner, an American agent for prisoners of war, sailed down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a> to the British fleet. Aboard HMS Tonnant on 7 September they secured Beanes's release from Major-General Robert Ross and Vice-Admiral Alexander Cochrane, but because they had overheard the plans for the attack on Baltimore they were held with the fleet until the battle was over. From their own vessel, under guard, Key watched the 25-hour bombardment of Fort McHenry that began at sunrise on 13 September. Between 1,500 and 1,800 shells and over 700 rockets were fired; four men died in the fort and 24 were wounded. On the morning of 14 September the large garrison flag, 15 stars and 15 stripes, made by Mary Young Pickersgill and other workers in her Baltimore home, was raised over the fort.</p><p>Key began his poem that morning on the back of a letter and finished it at the Indian Queen Hotel in Baltimore after his release on 16 September. Much of its idea and some of its wording came from his earlier song &quot;When the Warrior Returns&quot;, written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-decatur">Stephen Decatur</a> and Charles Stewart after the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> War and set to the same tune. His brother-in-law Joseph H. Nicholson saw that the words fitted &quot;The Anacreontic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; by the English composer John <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> Smith, the song of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> gentlemen's club. A Baltimore printer issued it as a broadside, &quot;Defence of Fort M'Henry&quot;, on 17 September; it was in 17 newspapers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> within weeks, and Thomas Carr of Baltimore published words and music together as &quot;The Star Spangled Banner&quot;. Its range of a twelfth makes it hard for most people to sing.</p><p>The song was played at public events through the 19th century. The Navy adopted it officially in 1889, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> ordered it played at military and other occasions in 1916, and a standard arrangement agreed by five musicians, among them Walter Damrosch and John Philip Sousa, was first performed at Carnegie Hall on 5 December 1917. From 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> Society of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Daughters of 1812 campaigned for it as the national anthem, and Congressman John Charles Linthicum introduced six unsuccessful bills between 1918 and 1929. A petition of the Veterans of Foreign Wars with five million signatures reached the House Judiciary Committee on 31 January 1930. The Senate passed the bill on 3 March 1931 and President Herbert Hoover signed it, making the song the national anthem. All four stanzas are the anthem, but only the first is usually sung.</p><p>The anthem has carried political meanings since the 1960s. José Feliciano's slow blues version at the 1968 World Series caused a nationwide uproar, and a week later Tommie Smith and John Carlos raised black-gloved fists on the medal podium at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> Olympics while it played. Jimi Hendrix's instrumental at Woodstock in 1969 became one of the best-known performances. Since the 1990s the anthem has been criticised over the third stanza, which mentions slaves, and over Key's own record: he held slaves for much of his life. Most recent historians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, among them Gene A. Smith, Marc Leepson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Roediger, read that line as aimed at enslaved people who escaped to the British, who offered them freedom and a place in the Corps of Colonial Marines; Key's biographer Mark Clague reads it as aimed at British forces and their collaborators, and notes that Key freed four of the seven slaves he inherited.</p><p>From 2016 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> 49ers quarterback Colin Kaepernick and other athletes knelt during the anthem in protest against police brutality and racism; in 2017 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-trump">Donald Trump</a> called for kneeling players to be fired. In November 2017 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a> asked Congress to remove the song as the national anthem. On 19 June 2020, after the murder of George Floyd, protesters toppled the statue of Key in San Francisco. In 2025, after Trump's tariff announcements and talk of annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, Canadian crowds booed the anthem at sporting events. Federal law (36 U.S.C. § 301) advises those present to stand facing the flag, hand over heart, or to salute if in the armed forces; the advice is not enforceable.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem in the voice of a watcher through a night of bombardment who asks at dawn whether the flag still flies, then sees it and rejoices. The later stanzas turn on the defeated invaders and end with trust in God as the nation's motto. The mood moves from anxious suspense to triumph.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O! say can you see by the dawn’s early light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>What so proudly we hailed at the twilight’s last gleaming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Whose broad stripes and bright stars through the perilous fight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O’er the ramparts we watch’d, were so gallantly streaming?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the Rockets’ red glare, the Bombs bursting in air,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Gave proof through the night that our Flag was still there;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O! say does that star-spangled Banner yet wave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O’er the Land of the free and the home of the brave?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On the shore dimly seen through the mists of the deep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the foe’s haughty host in dread silence reposes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>What is that which the breeze, o’er the towering steep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As it fitfully blows, half conceals, half discloses?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now it catches the gleam of the morning’s first beam,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In full glory reflected now shines on the stream,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>’Tis the star-spangled banner, O! long may it wave</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O’er the land of the free and the home of the brave.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And where is that band who so vauntingly swore</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That the havoc of war and the battle’s confusion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A home and a country should leave us no more?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their blood has washed out their foul footsteps pollution.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>No refuge could save the hireling and slave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From the terror of flight, or the gloom of the grave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the star-spangled banner in triumph doth wave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O’er the Land of the Free and the Home of the Brave.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O! thus be it ever, when freemen shall stand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Between their lov’d home and the war’s desolation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Blest with vict’ry and peace, may the Heav’n rescued land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Praise the Power that hath made and preserv’d us a nation!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then conquer we must, when our cause it is just,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And this be our motto—“In God is our Trust;”</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the star-spangled Banner in triumph shall wave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O’er the Land of the Free and the Home of the Brave.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Francis Scott Key; broadside of September 1814. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Defence_of_Fort_McHenry_(broadside)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="k6k0micCIkI" data-title="The Star-Spangled Banner: The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square (2013)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square (2013)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k6k0micCIkI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Defence of Fort M'Henry broadside, Baltimore</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1814</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first printing</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Star Spangled Banner (Thomas Carr's edition)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1814</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement with a raised fourth that became standard</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">German translation</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fifth stanza by Oliver Wendell Holmes Sr.</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">added verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Spanish-language version</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1919</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Standard version agreed by Damrosch, Earhart, Gantvoort, Sonneck and Sousa</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">official arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">José Feliciano, 1968 World Series</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1968</span><span class="sg-chain-h">blues rendition</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jimi Hendrix at Woodstock</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1969</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental rendition</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Whitney Houston, Super Bowl XXV</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1991</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording released as a single</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Latin American Spanish version sung in immigration reform protests</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">John Williams's arrangement for the bicentennial</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2014</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Anacreontic Song (John Stafford Smith)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">When the Warrior Returns (Francis Scott Key)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier song by Key on the same tune; source of the flag imagery and some wording</span></li></ul></section>
